Chapter: PciProbe
  cites Foreword chapter MemoryMap
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopQr
  cites Kernel chapter BitmapFont
  cites Works chapter GopHandoff

 A chipset-integrated part (the PCH's own Ethernet, SATA and xHCI) sits on
 bus 0. A discrete one sits behind a PCI-to-PCI bridge, on a bus whose
 number only that bridge knows, so bus 0 alone answers the question on
 some boards and silently answers nothing on others.

 We say:

Section: Halt

  pp-halt : Integer -> Integer
  pp-halt (n) = pp-halt (n + 1)

Section: Hex

  pp-nib : Integer -> Text
  pp-nib (n) =
    if n == 0 then "0" else if n == 1 then "1" else if n == 2 then "2"
    else if n == 3 then "3" else if n == 4 then "4" else if n == 5 then "5"
    else if n == 6 then "6" else if n == 7 then "7" else if n == 8 then "8"
    else if n == 9 then "9" else if n == 10 then "a" else if n == 11 then "b"
    else if n == 12 then "c" else if n == 13 then "d" else if n == 14 then "e"
    else "f"

  pp-hx : Integer, Integer -> Text
  pp-hx (v) (sh) = pp-nib (bit-and (bit-shru v sh) 15)

  pp-hex2 : Integer -> Text
  pp-hex2 (v) = pp-hx v 4 & pp-hx v 0

  pp-hex4 : Integer -> Text
  pp-hex4 (v) = pp-hx v 12 & pp-hx v 8 & pp-hx v 4 & pp-hx v 0

  pp-hex8 : Integer -> Text
  pp-hex8 (v) = pp-hex4 (bit-shru v 16) & pp-hex4 v

Section: Config Reads

  pp-cfg : PciDevice, Integer -> Integer
  pp-cfg (d) (off) = pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) off

 Register 8 carries the revision in its low byte and the programming
 interface in the next one up; the class and subclass above them are
 already on the record. Prog-if is what separates an AHCI controller from
 the same silicon in IDE mode, and xHCI (48) from EHCI (32).

  pp-progif : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-progif (d) = bit-and (bit-shru (pp-cfg d 8) 8) 255

  pp-rev : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-rev (d) = bit-and (pp-cfg d 8) 255

 Register 24 on a bridge holds the primary bus in its low byte and the
 SECONDARY bus in the next one: the number of the bus on the far side,
 which is the one to walk.

  pp-sec-bus : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-sec-bus (d) = bit-and (bit-shru (pp-cfg d 24) 8) 255

Section: The Bus Walk

  pp-collect : Integer, Integer, List PciDevice -> List PciDevice
  pp-collect (bus) (depth) (acc) =
    if depth > 3 then acc
    else let s = pci-scan-bus bus
    in pp-bridges (s.devices) (s.count) 0 bus depth (acc & s.devices)

  pp-bridges : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List PciDevice -> List PciDevice
  pp-bridges (devs) (n) (i) (bus) (depth) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if (list-at devs i).pci-header-type == 1 then pp-bridge-one (list-at devs i) devs n i bus depth acc
    else pp-bridges devs n (i + 1) bus depth acc

 A secondary bus at or below the bus being walked is either an unconfigured
 bridge or a malformed one, and following it would not terminate. The depth
 cap above is the second guard on the same hazard.

  pp-bridge-one : PciDevice, List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List PciDevice -> List PciDevice
  pp-bridge-one (d) (devs) (n) (i) (bus) (depth) (acc) =
    let sec = pp-sec-bus d
    in if sec > bus then pp-bridges devs n (i + 1) bus depth (pp-collect sec (depth + 1) acc)
    else pp-bridges devs n (i + 1) bus depth acc

Section: Classification

  pp-is-net : PciDevice -> Boolean
  pp-is-net (d) = d.pci-class == 2

  pp-is-storage : PciDevice -> Boolean
  pp-is-storage (d) = d.pci-class == 1

  pp-is-usb : PciDevice -> Boolean
  pp-is-usb (d) = if d.pci-class == 12 then d.pci-subclass == 3 else False

  pp-kind-of : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-kind-of (d) =
    if pp-is-net d then 2
    else if pp-is-storage d then 1
    else if pp-is-usb d then 12
    else 0

  pp-colour : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-colour (d) =
    if pp-is-net d then 16776960
    else if pp-is-storage d then 65535
    else if pp-is-usb d then 65280
    else 9474192

  pp-find : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-find (devs) (n) (i) (kind) =
    if i >= n then -1
    else if pp-kind-of (list-at devs i) == kind then i
    else pp-find devs n (i + 1) kind

Section: Formatting

  pp-bdf : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-bdf (d) = pp-hex2 (d.pci-bus) & ":" & pp-hex2 (d.pci-dev) & "." & show (d.pci-func)

  pp-ids : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-ids (d) = pp-hex4 (d.pci-vendor) & ":" & pp-hex4 (d.pci-device-id)

  pp-cls : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-cls (d) = pp-hex2 (d.pci-class) & "." & pp-hex2 (d.pci-subclass) & "." & pp-hex2 (pp-progif d)

  pp-line : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-line (d) = pp-bdf d & " " & pp-ids d & " " & pp-cls d

 The subsystem pair is what names the board variant of a part whose
 vendor:device is shared across a whole family, and the BARs are raw
 rather than parsed: a one-shot reading should carry what the register
 held, not this chapter's reading of it.

  pp-tail-a : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-tail-a (d) =
    let sub = pp-cfg d 44
    in " r" & pp-hex2 (pp-rev d) & " s" & pp-hex4 (bit-and sub 65535) & ":" & pp-hex4 (bit-shru sub 16)

 BAR0 is where a NIC and an xHCI put their register window, and BAR5 is
 where an AHCI controller puts its: on an ICH9 in AHCI mode BAR0 through
 BAR4 are the vestigial IDE bars and read back zero, so a reading that
 took BAR0 alone would report a storage controller at address nothing.
 BAR1 is carried because it holds the high half of a 64-bit BAR0.

  pp-tail-b : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-tail-b (d) =
    " i" & pp-hex2 (bit-and (pp-cfg d 60) 255) & " B0=" & pp-hex8 (pp-cfg d 16) & " B1=" & pp-hex8 (pp-cfg d 20) & " B5=" & pp-hex8 (pp-cfg d 36)

 Whether the register window is REACHABLE, which is a different question
 from where it is. The runtime page tables map exactly one gigabyte above
 RAM for devices, 3 GB to 4 GB. A BAR the firmware placed in that range
 can be read as soon as the device is enabled; a 64-bit BAR placed above
 4 GB is not mapped at all, and a driver would need another page
 directory before it could read a single register. Both are ordinary
 outcomes of firmware assignment, so the probe answers it rather than
 leaving the arithmetic to be done at the machine.

  pp-bar-base : PciDevice, Integer -> Integer
  pp-bar-base (d) (off) =
    let lo = pp-cfg d off
    in if bit-and lo 1 == 1 then bit-and lo 65532
    else if bit-and (bit-shru lo 1) 3 == 2 then bit-or (bit-and lo 4294967280) (bit-shl (bit-and (pp-cfg d (off + 4)) 4294967295) 32)
    else bit-and lo 4294967280

  pp-map-note : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-map-note (d) =
    let b = pp-bar-base d (if pp-is-storage d then 36 else 16)
    in if b == 0 then " MAP=none"
    else if b < bare-metal-device-window-lo then " MAP=BELOW3G"
    else if b < bare-metal-device-window-hi then " MAP=ok"
    else " MAP=ABOVE4G"

  pp-tail : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-tail (d) = pp-tail-a d & pp-tail-b d & pp-map-note d

Section: The Body

  pp-body : List PciDevice, Integer -> Text
  pp-body (devs) (n) = "PCIPROBE1 n=" & show n & ";" & pp-body-loop devs n 0 ""

  pp-body-loop : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  pp-body-loop (devs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let d = list-at devs i
    in let one = pp-line d & (if pp-kind-of d == 0 then "" else pp-tail d) & ";"
    in pp-body-loop devs n (i + 1) (acc & one)

Section: Rendering

  pp-row : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  pp-row (base) (stride) (font) (x) (y) (scale) (s) (col) =
    gop-draw-text base stride font x y s col scale 0

 gop-draw-text does not stop at the right edge: an overrun wraps to column
 zero of the row below and lands on top of whatever is there. That is why
 the identity and the detail are two lines at two scales rather than one.

  pp-callout : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Text, List PciDevice, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-callout (base) (stride) (font) (hs) (y) (label) (devs) (n) (kind) =
    let i = pp-find devs n 0 kind
    in if i < 0 then pp-row base stride font 40 y hs (label & "  NONE FOUND ON ANY BUS") 16711680
    else pp-callout-hit base stride font hs y label (list-at devs i)

  pp-callout-hit : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Text, PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-callout-hit (base) (stride) (font) (hs) (y) (label) (d) =
    let a = pp-row base stride font 40 y hs (label & "  " & pp-line d) (pp-colour d)
    in pp-row base stride font 40 (y + 20 * hs) 1 (pp-tail d) (pp-colour d)

  pp-list : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-list (base) (stride) (font) (devs) (n) (i) (y0) (rows) (lcols) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else if i >= rows * lcols then 0
    else let d = list-at devs i
    in let x = 40 + (i / rows) * 300
    in let t = pp-row base stride font x (y0 + (int-mod i rows) * 18) 1 (pp-line d) (pp-colour d)
    in pp-list base stride font devs n (i + 1) y0 rows lcols

Section: The Codes

 A module rendered two pixels wide does not survive a photograph: at
 scale 2 on a 1280x800 screen the decoder found the finder patterns and
 read none of the codes. So scale 2 is not offered, and the codes are
 sized before the screen is divided rather than squeezed into a fixed
 band afterwards: the block takes the largest of 6, 5, 4, 3 that fits
 the space below the callouts, and the device list gets whatever is
 left. A fixed half-screen band cost two scale steps and silently
 truncated the list to four rows on a six-device machine.

  pp-fits : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  pp-fits (w) (avail) (cn) (s) =
    let pitch = 45 * s + 24
    in let cols = (w - 40) / pitch
    in if cols < 1 then False
    else ((cn + cols - 1) / cols) * pitch <= avail

  pp-qh : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-qh (w) (cn) (s) =
    let pitch = 45 * s + 24
    in let cols = (w - 40) / pitch
    in if cols < 1 then 0
    else ((cn + cols - 1) / cols) * pitch

  pp-qs : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-qs (w) (avail) (cn) =
    if pp-fits w avail cn 6 then 6
    else if pp-fits w avail cn 5 then 5
    else if pp-fits w avail cn 4 then 4
    else 3

  pp-qr : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-qr (base) (stride) (w) (qy) (qs) (font) (body) (total) (cn) =
    let cols = (w - 40) / (45 * qs + 24)
    in let warn = if qs < 4 then "  SCALE 3: SHOOT CLOSE AND SQUARE" else ""
    in let t = pp-row base stride font 40 (qy - 22) 1 ("QR: " & show cn & " codes, " & show total & " bytes, scale " & show qs & ". Photograph every one." & warn) (if qs < 4 then 16776960 else 16777215)
    in pp-qr-chunks base stride qy qs cols body total cn 0

  pp-qr-chunks : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-qr-chunks (base) (stride) (qy) (qs) (cols) (body) (total) (cn) (i) =
    if i >= cn then 0
    else let from = i * 100
    in let len = if total - from > 100 then 100 else total - from
    in let m = qr5-encode-part (show (i + 1) & "/" & show cn & ";") body from len
    in let d = qr-draw base stride (40 + (int-mod i cols) * (45 * qs + 24)) (qy + (i / cols) * (45 * qs + 24)) qs m
    in pp-qr-chunks base stride qy qs cols body total cn (i + 1)

Section: The Screen

  pp-count-line : Integer, Integer -> Text
  pp-count-line (n) (shown) =
    if shown >= n then "devices=" & show n & "  all listed  bus 0 and every bridge below"
    else "devices=" & show n & "  ONLY " & show shown & " FIT ON SCREEN -- THE QR CODES CARRY ALL " & show n

  pp-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, List PciDevice, Integer -> Integer
  pp-draw (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (devs) (n) =
    let hs = if w >= 1024 then 2 else 1
    in let y0 = 100 + 3 * (24 * hs + 22) + 10
    in let body = pp-body devs n
    in let total = text-length body
    in let cn = (total + 99) / 100
    in let qs = pp-qs w (h - y0 - 40) cn
    in let qh = pp-qh w cn qs
    in let qyr = h - qh - 10
    in let qy = if qyr < y0 + 26 then y0 + 26 else qyr
    in let space = qy - 26 - y0
    in let rows = if space < 18 then 0 else space / 18
    in let lcols = (w - 40) / 300
    in let cap = rows * lcols
    in let shown = if n > cap then cap else n
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h 1710638
    in let t0 = pp-row base stride font 40 40 hs "PCI PROBE" 65535
    in let t1 = pp-row base stride font 40 70 hs (pp-count-line n shown) (if shown < n then 16711680 else 16777215)
    in let c1 = pp-callout base stride font hs 100 "NIC    " devs n 2
    in let c2 = pp-callout base stride font hs (100 + 24 * hs + 22) "STORAGE" devs n 1
    in let c3 = pp-callout base stride font hs (100 + 2 * (24 * hs + 22)) "USB    " devs n 12
    in let l = pp-list base stride font devs n 0 y0 rows lcols
    in pp-qr base stride w qy qs font body total cn

Section: Entry

  opening : [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Integer
  opening =
    let base = boot-fb-base 0
    in let w = boot-fb-w 0
    in let h = boot-fb-h 0
    in let stride = boot-fb-stride 0
    in let font = cbf-init
    in act
      ri <- runtime-init 0
      let devs = pp-collect 0 0 []
      in let d = pp-draw base w h stride font devs (list-length devs)
      in pp-halt 0
    end
